### LEARNING OBJECTIVES

Upon successful completion of this course, participants will be able to:

1. **Differentiate** between substance use, misuse, and substance use disorders using DSM-5-TR diagnostic criteria
2. **Explain** the neurobiological mechanisms underlying addiction, including the brain's reward system and neuroadaptation
3. **Apply** evidence-based screening and assessment tools for substance use disorders in clinical practice
4. **Implement** motivational interviewing techniques and other evidence-based interventions for clients with SUDs
5. **Integrate** relapse prevention strategies and recovery support into comprehensive treatment planning
6. **Evaluate** co-occurring disorders and develop integrated treatment approaches for dual diagnosis clients

---

### COURSE INTRODUCTION

Welcome to "28 Days Later: Understanding Addiction and Recovery." The title references both the common duration of residential treatment programs and the transformative journey that begins when clients commit to recovery. Just as the protagonists in survival narratives must navigate a fundamentally changed landscape, individuals recovering from addiction must learn to navigate their world with new awareness, skills, and support systems.

Substance use disorders represent one of the most significant public health challenges facing mental health professionals today. According to the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA, 2023), approximately 48.7 million Americans aged 12 and older met criteria for a substance use disorder in the past year. Despite this prevalence, only 10.3% of individuals with SUDs received any form of treatment, highlighting the critical need for trained professionals who can effectively identify, engage, and treat this population.

This course provides a comprehensive examination of addiction from neurobiological, psychological, and social perspectives. You will learn to apply evidence-based assessment and intervention strategies while developing cultural humility in working with diverse populations affected by substance use. Throughout our modules, you will follow two continuing case studies—**Derek** and **Marisol**—whose clinical presentations will help you apply theoretical concepts to real-world practice.

