---

## Course Information

**Course Title:** Motivational Interviewing — From Ambivalence to Action
**CE Hours:** 3 (Category: Core)
**Instructional Level:** Intermediate
**Target Audience:** Licensed Professional Counselors, Licensed Clinical Social Workers, Licensed Marriage and Family Therapists, Licensed Psychologists, and other behavioral health professionals
**Course Format:** Self-paced online text-based instruction with embedded assessments

---

## Course Description

This comprehensive continuing education course provides mental health professionals with an in-depth exploration of Motivational Interviewing (MI), the evidence-based, client-centered counseling approach developed by William R. Miller and Stephen Rollnick. MI has become one of the most widely researched and empirically supported therapeutic approaches in behavioral health, with applications spanning substance use treatment, chronic disease management, health behavior change, criminal justice settings, and general psychotherapy. This course moves beyond introductory familiarity with MI to provide clinicians with the clinical depth necessary to implement MI with fidelity, integrate MI with other therapeutic modalities, and apply MI principles across diverse clinical populations and settings. Participants will gain advanced understanding of the theoretical foundations of MI, master the micro-skills that constitute competent MI practice, learn to recognize and strategically respond to change talk and sustain talk, and develop the ability to navigate complex clinical scenarios including ambivalence about change, co-occurring disorders, and treatment resistance. The course includes extensive clinical vignettes, decision-point exercises, and practical application scenarios designed to bridge the gap between conceptual understanding and clinical implementation.

---

## Learning Objectives

Upon completion of this course, participants will be able to:

1. Articulate the theoretical foundations of Motivational Interviewing, including the spirit of MI, its roots in humanistic psychology, self-determination theory, and the transtheoretical model of change.
2. Identify and demonstrate competency in the four core processes of MI: engaging, focusing, evoking, and planning.
3. Apply the OARS micro-skills (Open questions, Affirmations, Reflections, and Summaries) with clinical precision across diverse client presentations.
4. Differentiate between change talk and sustain talk, recognizing subcategories of each, and employ evidence-based strategies to elicit and reinforce change talk.
5. Integrate MI with other evidence-based treatment approaches including Cognitive Behavioral Therapy, Dialectical Behavior Therapy, and trauma-informed care models.
6. Adapt MI techniques for diverse populations including adolescents, older adults, individuals with co-occurring mental health and substance use disorders, and clients from diverse cultural backgrounds.
7. Identify common clinician traps and righting reflexes that undermine the MI process and implement strategies to avoid them.
8. Evaluate clinical scenarios using MI-consistent decision-making frameworks and determine appropriate interventions.
9. Utilize MI fidelity measures, including the Motivational Interviewing Treatment Integrity (MITI) coding system, to assess and improve personal MI competence.
10. Apply ethical considerations specific to the practice of MI, including informed consent, cultural responsiveness, and managing dual relationships in motivational enhancement contexts.

