## Course Description

This comprehensive course examines the ethical foundations essential to professional counseling practice, with particular emphasis on the 2014 ACA Code of Ethics and its practical applications in contemporary clinical settings. Participants will explore systematic approaches to ethical decision-making, develop nuanced understanding of professional boundaries, and examine the complex intersections of confidentiality, dual relationships, and mandated reporting requirements. Through case studies, self-reflection exercises, and practical scenarios, counselors will strengthen their capacity to navigate the ethical challenges inherent in therapeutic relationships while maintaining the highest standards of professional conduct.

---

## Learning Objectives

Upon successful completion of this course, participants will be able to:

1. Identify and articulate the core ethical principles that form the foundation of professional counseling practice, including autonomy, nonmaleficence, beneficence, justice, fidelity, and veracity.

2. Apply at least two systematic ethical decision-making models (Kitchener's model, Forester-Miller & Davis model, or Wheeler & Bertram's model) to complex clinical scenarios.

3. Differentiate between boundary crossings and boundary violations, and evaluate clinical situations using established criteria for determining appropriateness of boundary decisions.

4. Analyze confidentiality requirements and their exceptions, including mandated reporting obligations, duty to warn provisions, and HIPAA considerations in counseling practice.

5. Evaluate dual relationship scenarios using ethical guidelines and develop strategies for managing unavoidable multiple relationships in professional practice.

6. Develop a comprehensive personal ethical practice plan that addresses proactive strategies for maintaining ethical standards throughout one's career.

---

