## Course Description

Suicide remains one of the leading causes of death in the United States, claiming approximately 49,500 lives annually and affecting millions more through attempts, ideation, and bereavement. Mental health professionals occupy a critical position in suicide prevention, yet many report feeling inadequately prepared to assess and intervene with suicidal clients. This comprehensive course provides counselors with evidence-based frameworks for understanding suicidal behavior, conducting thorough risk assessments, implementing effective crisis interventions, and managing the complex ethical-legal considerations surrounding suicide care. Through examination of contemporary research, validated assessment instruments, and practical intervention strategies, participants will develop the competence and confidence necessary to work effectively with clients experiencing suicidal crises.

---

## Learning Objectives

Upon successful completion of this course, participants will be able to:

1. Analyze current epidemiological data on suicide, including prevalence rates, demographic patterns, and means of death, to inform culturally responsive assessment and intervention practices.

2. Apply theoretical models of suicidal behavior, including Joiner's Interpersonal-Psychological Theory, to understand the psychological mechanisms underlying suicide risk.

3. Conduct comprehensive suicide risk assessments utilizing validated instruments including the Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS) and evidence-based clinical interviewing techniques.

4. Implement the Stanley-Brown Safety Planning Intervention and other evidence-based crisis intervention strategies with clients across risk levels.

5. Apply lethal means counseling protocols to reduce access to methods of suicide, particularly firearms, as a critical component of comprehensive risk management.

6. Identify suicide risk factors, warning signs, and protective factors specific to high-risk populations including adolescents, older adults, veterans, and LGBTQ+ individuals.

7. Navigate the ethical and legal obligations surrounding suicide care, including duty to protect, informed consent, documentation requirements, and involuntary hospitalization procedures.

8. Develop and implement self-care strategies to address vicarious traumatization and manage the emotional impact of working with suicidal clients.

